--- ON THE GUARD-ORDERING ASSERTION (AC-005 sub-case (b), security-spec B3) ---
Cases 3 and 4 above are the assertions security-spec.md B3's STRIDE row
requires: the ones that must NOT go green if the isAllowlisted/isDenylisted
check is moved inside the new errors-collecting try/catch. They are written so
that a swallowed guard check cannot satisfy them, and this is a property of
the assertions themselves, not of the RED capture:

  * both fixtures are REAL, READABLE, NON-EMPTY directories (case 3:
    plugins/some-plugin holding script.sh and nested/notes.md; case 4:
    reports/.env holding leak.txt), so a walk that ran would return entries;
  * each asserts `files` deep-equals [] AND that no entry name from inside the
    denied directory ("script.sh", "notes.md", "leak.txt") appears anywhere in
    JSON.stringify(result) -- a name could only get there via a walk;
  * each asserts errors[0].reason EQUALS the guard's own hard-deny message
    ("Path is outside the allowlisted directories." /
    "Path matches a denylisted file.") and does NOT match /ENOENT|EACCES|
    EPERM|ENOTDIR/, so a denial reinterpreted as an I/O error fails;
  * case 3 additionally runs a POSITIVE CONTROL of the identical directory
    shape under an allowlisted parent (reports/control-plugin) and asserts the
    walk DOES list it -- so `files: []` on the denied path cannot be an
    artefact of an unwalkable fixture.

